        "According to the Open ASI Governance Forum's deficiency register, entry D-56 states: \"The local arm recorded that thinking was 'disabled structurally by grammar constraint'. It was not, one sample in five.\"\n\nThe register details the following about D-56:\n\n*   **Origin:** It was first articulated by the annotator on August 8, 2026, based on \"a three-arm controlled experiment on the round-012 prompt, run after the same halt had fired in four rounds and been tolerated each time.\"\n*   **Forward Control:** The required control is to compare a recorded `serve_configuration` against the request body that produced it. The register notes that a field stating an intention rather than the setting actually sent is \"unfalsifiable by inspection,\" and this false state was believed for four rounds.\n*   **Affected Objects:**\n    *   `tools/solicit_local.py`: This is \"repairable by supersession\" with remediation \"partly applied.\" The tool now explicitly sends `chat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking=false`. Testing showed a ~14x reduction (1/120 truncations with the flag versus 14/120 without), which is a reduction but *not* an elimination. The register also corrects an earlier note that falsely claimed a 0/20 elimination, noting that the earlier test was underpowered and measured a different server than the round actually uses.\n    *   `corpus/raw/round-002 … round-012 qwen samples`: This is \"not repairable\" (remediation is \"impossible\"). Every one of these samples carries the false `reasoning_effort` string in its committed `serve_configuration`. Because raw files are never edited after being committed, the deficiency register entry itself is the only thing that contradicts them.": 1,
